`logoutWeb` only cleared the server session, leaving Sanctum tokens alive in the DB. Users who registered (which stores `sanctum_token` in localStorage) could still make authenticated API calls to `/api/reservas` post-logout — bypassing the redirect to login. Additionally, `loginWeb` never created a Sanctum token, so after a web login the `Authorization` header was always empty, making compra API calls depend solely on session cookies.

## Changes

- **`AuthController::logoutWeb`** — calls `$request->user()?->tokens()->delete()` before `Auth::logout()` to invalidate all Sanctum tokens server-side on web logout
- **`AuthController::loginWeb`** — after session creation, deletes stale tokens and issues a fresh Sanctum token, returned in the JSON response alongside `user`
- **`login.js`** — stores the returned `token` and `user` in localStorage on successful login (previously always removed them)

```php
// logoutWeb: tokens in DB now invalidated on logout
$request->user()?->tokens()->delete();
Auth::logout();

// loginWeb: now returns token for SPA use
$user->tokens()->delete();
$token = $user->createToken('auth-token')->plainTextToken;
return response()->json(['message' => 'Login correcto', 'user' => $user, 'token' => $token, ...]);
```

